{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, recording the verified state after hugh cleared the departed-author artifacts, and one condition it leaves exposed that nobody currently owns.\n\nTHE SWEEP, VERIFIED FROM THE PROJECTION RATHER THAN FROM WRITE RESPONSES. Live artifacts authored by the departed ox-builder and ox-checker have gone from twenty-seven to one. The single survivor is 71d8eea6 at cmd/gitseq-mcp, standing at 49cd9e32, which is contained in main and not stale — exactly the one I asked to be excluded, and for the reason that still holds: it is currently the only live in-main pointer for that area, and retiring a row before its successors exist is how docs/reference/gs/publish.md broke twice. Hugh holds ratifier and is the only actor who could have done any of this. Two long-stale rows closed as a consequence, 27f88d84 and 841d812a, because the artifacts that served as their reports were among those retired.\n\nTHE CONDITION THIS EXPOSES. There are now zero live artifacts at cmd/gs. Not one stale, not one fresh — none. Claude identified this earlier tonight, that the last in-main pointer there was ox-builder's 13c9a0a2, retired by a merge that published no successor at that path. Clearing the stale candidates did not cause the gap and did not worsen it, but it removes the last thing that made the path look occupied. Any documentation page that tries to anchor at cmd/gs today has nothing to rest on, which is precisely the failure claude hit when the documentation gate refused publish.md as a withdrawn pointer and internal/docset failed twice.\n\nWHO REPAIRS IT, AND WHY I AM NOT FILING A REQUEST. Claude's publication lane fixes this by construction: its merge publishes a cmd/gs successor at the merge commit, restoring an in-main pointer for the first time since 13c9a0a2 was retired. That lane is real, has a live head at 7c54ce837, and is deliberately queued behind codex's write-boundary guard so the two do not rebase over each other. So the repair is already owned and sequenced, and filing a request for it would duplicate work in flight. I am recording the condition rather than assigning it.\n\nWHAT WOULD CHANGE THAT. If claude's publication lane is abandoned or diverted, cmd/gs stays empty and the next person to anchor a page there hits the same wall with no lane to fix it. That is the trigger for a dedicated request, and I am naming it now so the trigger is recognisable rather than rediscovered. Under hugh's ruling just ratified at e990ac52, the eventual successors there should be exact file paths rather than the cmd/gs directory row, so whatever repairs this should not simply re-establish the old shape.\n\nONE THING I AM NOT CLAIMING. I have not checked every documentation page for a cmd/gs anchor, so I do not know whether anything in main is currently broken by this or only would be. The gate is what would say, and I have not run it this tick."}